From 96e7f99015a84a8b55975369e6daf9830eeef7f4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 13:47:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Include search: a directory that keeps failing for a
 subdirectory is asked for it, once (#2873)

glibc's headers include one another as <bits/...>, <sys/...>, <gnu/...>,
<linux/...> and <asm/...>, and each such include is probed in every
search directory before the one that has it: clang's resource directory
and /usr/local/include have no `bits`. On the 9700X those are 40 of the
52 failed opens of a unit with only <stdio.h>, and 167 of sqlite3.c's
207. A listing does not reach them: a small unit never gets a first
look, and clang's directory is read whole only at its 256th miss.

A failed probe of a path with more than one component after its
directory now counts for that first component, in one of 16 slots of the
directory's record. At a component's third miss the cache stats the
component's own path, once. A directory without it then gives ENOENT for
every path under it without a system call, as a listing does; one that
has it answers nothing, and its paths are opened as before. The ask is
the lookup every path under the component begins with, so it is right on
every file system, those a listing can't answer for among them.

Every failed probe is counted, in units that never ask too, so the
counting has no loop: a component's key is 16 bytes, its name and zeros,
by one masked load of the bytes after the directory's, and its slot is
one compare of the key with all 16 slots, four a vector. As two loops it
was 1.6 to 2 times the instructions and 2.4 to 3.1 times the branches
that a musl unit adds on the 9700X. The baseline host runs the same code
by its emulated intrinsics.

The third miss and a stat, from the traces of compiles without asks and
the calls' costs on the 9700X: asked at the second miss, musl's units,
whose components fail two or three times and are often there, lose 2,900
cycles each; at the third, 600, where the unit with <stdio.h> keeps
33,400 of 34,000 and sqlite3.c 162,900 of 170,000. Measured, one
executable asking at the third, the fourth and the fifth miss reads
alike.

On the 9700X, with the next commit's start order:
- The unit with <stdio.h> makes 59 opens for 87 and 24 failed ones for
  52, with 2 asks. One executable with its asks on reads 0.85% and 1.07%
  fewer kernel cycles than with them off (41 rounds each; off against
  off: 0.05%), and the build 1.11% and 0.89% fewer than main's.
- sqlite3.c makes 274 opens for 380 and 101 failed ones for 207, with 11
  asks. Its cycles read as main against a copy of itself.
- musl's 1,336 units, a process each, make 850 asks that spare 86 opens
  of 70,664. One executable reads the same with its asks on and off
  (0.01% and 0.04% more kernel cycles, 11 rounds each). The counting is
  0.03% more instructions than main. The build reads 0.10% and 0.14%
  more kernel cycles and 0.17% and 0.07% more user cycles than main's,
  where main against a copy of itself reads 0.15% and 0.07% fewer.
The objects are the same bytes.

Tests: test-preprocessor's directory asks (28 groups of searches, each
group's opens, asks and answers pinned, and every path, file and error
the same as with the asks off), its component keys (each path ending
where an unreadable page begins) and test-files's walk on every lane
count.

Part of #2873.

